From: Overlooked Arcade
Date: 2026-05-16
Subject: zutil.h — skip "fdopen=NULL" redefinition on modern macOS / __APPLE__

Upstream zlib 1.2.11 (Jan 2017) carries a TARGET_OS_MAC branch that defines
`fdopen(fd,mode)` to `NULL` on Mac unless built with CodeWarrior (`__MWERKS__`).
This dates from the old Mac OS Classic / pre-OSX toolchain where fdopen wasn't
always available.

On modern Apple platforms (macOS 10+, iOS, etc.), `<TargetConditionals.h>`
defines `TARGET_OS_MAC=1` AND `__APPLE__=1`. The redefinition kicks in, and
when zlib's other .c files later `#include <stdio.h>`, the system header's
declaration:

    FILE *fdopen(int, const char *) __DARWIN_ALIAS_STARTING(...);

gets macro-expanded into

    FILE *NULL(int, const char *) __DARWIN_ALIAS_STARTING(...);

which clang rejects with "expected identifier or '('".

This patch adds `!defined(__APPLE__)` to the inner guard so modern Apple
platforms skip the redefinition. The MWERKS branch (real Mac OS Classic builds)
is unchanged.

--- a/deps/zlib-1.2.11/zutil.h
+++ b/deps/zlib-1.2.11/zutil.h
@@ -133,7 +133,7 @@
 #  ifndef Z_SOLO
 #    if defined(__MWERKS__) && __dest_os != __be_os && __dest_os != __win32_os
 #      include <unix.h> /* for fdopen */
-#    else
+#    elif !defined(__APPLE__) /* OA patch: modern macOS provides fdopen via <stdio.h>; skip the NULL redefinition that breaks system header parsing on Xcode 15+. See PATCHES/0001-*.patch */
 #      ifndef fdopen
 #        define fdopen(fd,mode) NULL /* No fdopen() */
 #      endif
